{"step_index":4909,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T13:48:14Z","thinking":"Reviewing the system prompt again to ensure adherence to the critical rule of strict brand identity matching. The prompt emphasizes matching the exact commercial trade brand name, excluding generics or alternatives. This confirms the initial understanding and the need for precise matching criteria.\n\n\nThe system requires exact brand name matching, ruling out generics and alternatives, including those with prefixes or suffixes. OCR errors like dropped letters or phonetic variations of the *same* brand are acceptable. Dosage forms must be compatible; tablets cannot be matched with other forms, and so on.\n\n\nFocusing on dosage form compatibility, the system explicitly dictates distinct matching rules for each type; for instance, tablets must exclusively match tablets, and syrups must match only syrups, and so on. Combination products containing suffixes such as \"Co\" or \"Plus\" are prohibited from matching plain products. Multi-strength formulations also have strict non-matching criteria with single strengths.\n\n\nStrength matching is critical; conflicting strengths result in a \"no_match\" status. However, a missing strength in the warehouse listing defaults to a match if the drug and form are the same. Fractional and vernacular equivalencies like \"1gm = 1000mg\" need to be considered. Packaging and quantity differences are irrelevant for a match, provided the brand, form, and strength align.\n\n\nDifferences in strip counts, retail/wholesale packaging, or discrepancies between strips and total tablets are deemed a match. Missing pack counts or quantities on either side are also a match. Manufacturer suffixes and commercial indicators do not affect the product match.\nThe \"review\" safety net is crucial for manual inspection of ambiguous or borderline cases, preventing the loss of potentially available medications.\n\n\n","tool_calls":